What is anagen effluvium? :: Abrupt, diffuse shedding of hairs in the active growing (anagen) phase due to impaired follicular mitotic activity.
What is the primary cellular target damaged in anagen effluvium? :: Rapidly dividing matrix cells of the hair follicle bulb.
Why is anagen effluvium usually reversible rather than scarring? :: The quiescent follicular bulge stem cells are preserved despite destruction of the matrix.
What is the typical time interval between the causative insult and hair shedding in anagen effluvium? :: 1 to 3 weeks (typically 7 to 14 days).
What is the most common etiology of anagen effluvium? :: Systemic chemotherapy (e.g., antimetabolites, alkylating agents, taxanes).
Which heavy metal toxicities are classically associated with anagen effluvium? :: Thallium, arsenic, and bismuth.
What autoimmune blistering disorder can cause dystrophic anagen hair loss due to anti-desmoglein targeting of follicles? :: Pemphigus vulgaris.
What morphological feature characterizes the proximal end of a shed hair in anagen effluvium? :: Tapered, narrowed, or fractured dystrophic base (pencil-point or frayed end), lacking a root sheath.
What are Pohl-Pinkus constrictions? :: Irregular focal narrowings along the hair shaft caused by temporary metabolic inhibition of the hair matrix.
What microscopic/trichoscopic feature signifies hairs fractured at the scalp surface in anagen effluvium? :: Black dots (cadaverized hairs).
How does the onset of anagen effluvium differ chronologically from telogen effluvium? :: Anagen effluvium manifests rapidly in 1–3 weeks; telogen effluvium manifests 2–4 months post-trigger.
How does the hair root morphology differ between anagen effluvium and telogen effluvium on a pull test? :: Anagen effluvium yields dystrophic, tapered, non-bulbous roots; telogen effluvium yields rounded, depigmented club hairs.
How does the extent of shedding typically differ between anagen effluvium and telogen effluvium? :: Anagen effluvium often causes severe, near-total or complete alopecia (including eyebrows and eyelashes); telogen effluvium rarely exceeds 30–50% overall scalp thinning.
What percentage of normal scalp hairs are vulnerable to anagen effluvium at any given time? :: Approximately 85% to 90% (the normal anagen proportion of scalp follicles).
Is anagen effluvium classified as a cicatricial or non-cicatricial alopecia? :: Non-cicatricial (non-scarring) alopecia.
What non-pharmacological, physical intervention can decrease the severity of chemotherapy-induced anagen effluvium? :: Scalp cooling (scalp hypothermia) via vasoconstriction and decreased metabolic uptake.
When does spontaneous hair regrowth typically initiate after cessation of the offending cytotoxic agent? :: Within 1 to 3 months.
Can the texture or pigmentation of regrown hair change after recovery from anagen effluvium? :: Yes; hair may temporarily or permanently regrow with altered curliness, texture, or color.
What finding on a forceful hair pull test indicates active anagen effluvium? :: Painless extraction of numerous dystrophic anagen hairs with fractured, tapered proximal ends.
How does the distribution of alopecia areata totalis differ clinically from diffuse anagen effluvium? :: Alopecia areata typically begins as discrete, circumscribed patches before progressing to confluence, whereas anagen effluvium begins as immediate, diffuse thinning.